Hi all,

I wanted to add a perspective particularly on the delayed retry semantics. W=
e=E2=80=99ve been running exactly this pattern in production for the past 5 y=
ears handling delays from 2min to 10+ hours and it=E2=80=99s worked pretty w=
ell for us with just client changes and no broker config/updates whatsoever.=


This also aligns with Matthias=E2=80=99s point. The approach works because o=
f Kafka=E2=80=99s log properties, not in spite of them, so the broker stays u=
ntouched + EOS, zero-copy are fully preserved.
The key property we rely on is that partition offsets are monotonically incr=
easing with time of production. That means we only ever need to check the he=
ad of the partition if it=E2=80=99s not ready, it follows that nothing behin=
d it will be either.

Rather than encoding delay per-message, the delay is a property of the consu=
mer configuration for a given topic. We maintain multiple retry topics, each=
 representing a delay tier e.g. retry-0, retry-1, retry-2. Failed messages a=
re routed to the appropriate tier up to R_k times (configurable per k topic)=
. Each message carries one header: lastAttemptedAt, in addition to the sourc=
e topic/cluster/client/partition/offset info for retry routing/logging.

So essentially checkDelay becomes now - lastAttemptedAt >=3D configuredDelay=
ForThisTopic (retry-0/1/2 being something like 2m, 1h, 10h). If it=E2=80=99s=
 too soon to process, we pause the partition and resume after the remaining d=
elay, checked between polls. Rebalances are also pretty much ok if a paused p=
artition gets reassigned the new partition will do the same checkDelay+pause=
 step.

Retry topics can either be global kitchen sink or per-topic. Messages are go=
ing to be out of band but you can still choose to reuse the same partition k=
ey so in-partition order for the same keys are preserved. You=E2=80=99ll nee=
d a (de)serialization story for each message otherwise you=E2=80=99re only a=
ble to move around Consumer<byte[], byte[]>. After consuming you can choose t=
o reprocess in the retry poller threads or forward to the original/other sou=
rce topic. Usual challenges around creating new consumer groups, ACLs around=
 writing to them also apply.

Some benefits to this approach:

1. No broker changes. Offset monotonicity, EOS, zero-copy are fully preserve=
d
2. No cap on delay length. Same mechanic works for 30 seconds or 24 hours
3. Dynamic delay configuration. The delay lives in consumer config, not the m=
essage. Changing it takes effect immediately for all in-flight messages in t=
hat topic which is operationally useful during incidents or when dealing wit=
h large lag.

Since the poll() loop is the scheduler effectively, you don=E2=80=99t need a=
n external store to keep track of delay, everything is configured either on t=
he record header or the topic configuration.

One thing to note here: you can=E2=80=99t use this pattern as a general sche=
duler so it doesn=E2=80=99t suit =E2=80=9Cdeliver at this wall clock time=E2=
=80=9D scenario.=20

Happy to discuss any detail in particular.

Thanks,
Aditya Kousik

