> Jun Rao via dev <dev@kafka.apache.org> =E6=96=BC 2026=E5=B9=B44=E6=9C=8817=
=E6=97=A5 =E5=87=8C=E6=99=A84:57 =E5=AF=AB=E9=81=93=EF=BC=9A
>=20
> For Scenario 2: Log Truncation Data Loss, the to_start_time semantic seems=

> weird to me. A common scenario is that a consumer application has been
> stopped for longer than the data retention time and the committed offsets
> are out of range. A consumer in this state doesn't seem much different fro=
m
> a consumer being started for the first time. It seems inconsistent that we=

> use the latest offset for consumption in one case and the earliest in
> another

The core semantic of to_start_time is to read all records since the creation=
 of the group. Hence, the =E2=80=9Cinconsistent=E2=80=9D behavior you mentio=
ned is actually just the strictly consistent application of the group's crea=
tion time.


