Hello Jun,

Thank for the feedback,

Adding to the points above:

Regarding by_duration as an alternative to Scenario 1: beyond clock skew=20=

and retry issues, there is also a usability concern. by_duration =
requires users
to reason about operational timing =E2=80=94 "how long does partition =
discovery take=20
in my environment?=E2=80=9D, and then translate that into a =
configuration value. to_start_time=20
requires no such reasoning. It simply anchors to the group creation time =
recorded=20
by the broker.

Regarding Scenario 2: I'd also like to clarify that to_start_time does =
not branch between=20
"use latest" and "use earliest." It applies the same ListOffsetsRequest =
with the group creation=20
timestamp in all cases. The difference in outcome:
- skipping old data on first start
- consuming surviving data after truncation
is a natural consequence of what data exists in the partition at that =
point, not a different policy=20
being applied. The rule is always the same.

Best Regards,
Jiunn-Yang

> Chia-Ping Tsai <chia7712@gmail.com> =E6=96=BC 2026=E5=B9=B44=E6=9C=8817=E6=
=97=A5 =E4=B8=8A=E5=8D=889:48 =E5=AF=AB=E9=81=93=EF=BC=9A
>=20
>=20
>> Jun Rao via dev <dev@kafka.apache.org> =E6=96=BC 2026=E5=B9=B44=E6=9C=88=
17=E6=97=A5 =E5=87=8C=E6=99=A84:57 =E5=AF=AB=E9=81=93=EF=BC=9A
>>=20
>> Also, a group is deleted after the consumer has been idle longer
>> than offsets.retention.minutes. What's the semantic of to_start_time =
if the
>> group creation time is unavailable?
>=20
> If the group is recreated, a new creation time will be recorded. =
Hence, it acts like a new group. Plus, it throws an exception directly =
if the group truly has no creation time.


