>=20
> I=E2=80=99ve published KIP-1310: General Transaction Session and would like=
 to open the floor for discussion.
>=20
> Historically, Kafka=E2=80=99s transaction logic has been locked inside Kafk=
aProducer. This worked well for simple "write-and-commit" patterns, but as th=
e ecosystem matures, this monolithic design has become a hurdle. We now have =
multiple entities=C2=A0e.g.=C2=A0Flink/External Coordinators, Share Group Con=
sumers (KIP-1289),=C2=A0Kafka Connect=E2=80=94that need to participate in or =
complete transactions without needing the heavy baggage of a full KafkaProduc=
er (record batching, serializers, sender threads, etc.).
>=20
> KIP-1310 extracts transaction identity and lifecycle management into a firs=
t-class TransactionSession client.
>=20
> Proposal:
>   =20
>    -   =20
> New TransactionSession Class: A lightweight, thread-safe object for identit=
y (producerId/epoch) and lifecycle (initialize, beginTransaction, commit).
>=20
>    -   =20
> Reflection-Free Recovery: Replaces reflection workarounds with a public Tra=
nsactionSession.resume() API for external coordinators.
>=20
>    -   =20
> Decoupled Architecture: Allows KafkaProducer and KafkaShareConsumer to shar=
e a single transaction identity for atomic "Exactly-Once" Kafka-to-Kafka pipe=
lines (KIP-1302).
>=20
>    -   =20
> Automatic Heartbeats: Provides a dedicated home for the background transact=
ion heartbeat (KAFKA-20381), independent of the producer's data-sending loop.
>=20
>=20
> This change is fully backward compatible and introduces no new wire protoco=
l changes=E2=80=94it simply aligns the client API with what the Kafka protoco=
l already supports.
>=20
> I=E2=80=99m looking forward to your feedback on the proposed interfaces and=
 the refactoring of TransactionManager.
> KIP Link:=C2=A0https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/x/nJY8G=C2=A0
> Best regards,Shekharhttps://github.com/Shekharrajak=C2=A0
>=20
>=20
