Hi Lianet,

On LM1: In my mind, we already provide two different APIs of setting a =
ConsumerRebalanceListener:
the two subscribe() methods one with listener arg and the one without. =
Users can currently initialize
a listener in the first pass and then overwrite it with calls to the =
same subscribe() method. SubscriptionState
compares the new topic pattern and ignores it if nothing changes, but it =
registers the new listener=20
overriding the old one. I think this was Andrew=E2=80=99s original point =
about deprecating the method and=20
to not support both overrides at the same time.=20

A similar argument is done for removing a listener: currently you can =
invoke subscribe(Collection)
or you can call subscribe(Collection, null) listener which both achieve =
the same thing. So, the new
setConsumerRebalanceListener method that we introduce is another facet =
to achieve the same=20
behaviour. I agree that it feels like migration done only halfway if =
nothing changes, but I think we followed
the same approach for removing checksum from consumer record in the =
constructor until AK3.0.=20

For all 4.4.+, the migration is essentially changing all references in =
JavaDoc away from the no-listener
method and pointing to setConsumerRebalanceListener and the hard blocker =
will be when clients
upgrade to 5.0 and their compilation fails because of the removed =
method. At that point, users who
have noticed the documentation change will have already moved, but those =
